<p>A 16:9 video sits <em>in</em> a post. A 9:16 clip <strong>is</strong> the post on a phone — one viewport, the clip, its title and who made it. On a desk the same clip goes left and the post goes right. There are no like, share or "more" buttons anywhere: they belong to a feed of strangers, and this is a blog.</p>

<h2>The page</h2>
<p>The clip on the left at its real proportion, previous and next beside it, and on the right the post: title, byline, body, facts, one comment — vertically centred against the clip. One viewport; the panel owns the only scrollbar. Loaded in an iframe because that is the only honest way to show a layout whose claim is "it fits".</p>
{{#example flush=true code=false}}
<iframe class="demo-frame" src="/demos/shortwatch/" title="A vertical clip with its post beside it" style="height: 40rem" loading="lazy"></iframe>
{{/example}}
<ul>
	<li><strong>Clip left, words right.</strong> A 9:16 clip on a 16:9 screen leaves two thirds of the window empty; the post lives there.</li>
	<li><strong>Up and down, beside the clip.</strong> The only controls. No reactions — this is a blog, not a feed of strangers.</li>
	<li><strong>On a phone the clip is a 16:9 head</strong> and the post follows underneath; the window scrolls.</li>
</ul>

<h2>On a phone</h2>
<p>One viewport, the clip and its title. Sized from both constraints at once so nothing overflows.</p>
{{#example flush=true code=false}}
<iframe class="demo-frame" src="/demos/short/" title="A vertical clip on a phone" style="height: 40rem; max-width: 24rem; margin-inline: auto; display: block" loading="lazy"></iframe>
{{/example}}

<h2>The other two 9:16 layouts</h2>
<p>One clip is a page; several are a <strong>feed</strong>; and on a desk, where there is room beside the clip, it is a <strong>watch page</strong> — the same viewport, split.</p>

<h3>One column that snaps</h3>
<p>One short is a page. Several are a <strong>feed</strong> — the same page repeated down a snapping column, where the scroll <em>is</em> the navigation. Scroll inside the frame.</p>
{{#example flush=true code=false}}
<iframe class="demo-frame" src="/demos/shorts/" title="A feed of vertical videos" style="height: 40rem" loading="lazy"></iframe>
{{/example}}
{{{{code "css"}}}}
.im-shortfeed {
	height: 100dvh;
	overflow-y: auto;
	scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
	overscroll-behavior-y: contain;
}

.im-shortfeed > .im-short {
	height: 100%;
	scroll-snap-align: start;
	scroll-snap-stop: always;
}
{{{{/code}}}}
<ul>
	<li><strong><code>scroll-snap-stop: always</code> is the whole feel of it.</strong> Without it a hard flick skips three clips, which is the one thing a feed must never do — a viewer who cannot land on the clip they aimed at stops aiming.</li>
	<li><strong><code>overscroll-behavior-y: contain</code></strong> stops the last clip handing the scroll back to the page behind it.</li>
	<li><strong>No next button.</strong> The gesture is the button; <code>im-short-step</code> belongs on the single page, where there is nothing to scroll.</li>
	<li><strong>Only the first poster is eager.</strong> Four autoplaying videos is four videos downloading at once — in a real feed, play the snapped one and pause the rest with one <code>IntersectionObserver</code> on the column.</li>
	<li><strong>Each clip is a landmark</strong> (<code>&lt;section aria-label="Short 2 of 4"&gt;</code>), so a screen-reader user knows where they are in a list that has no visible list.</li>
	<li><code>im-shortfeed-inset</code> when there is a top bar above the column instead of it filling the window.</li>
</ul>

<h2>In a grid</h2>
<p>A vertical card is the same <a href="/collections/video/card/">video card</a> with one variable changed — <code>im-vidcard-tall</code> sets <code>--im-vidcard-ratio: 9 / 16</code>. Not a second component.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-vidgrid" style="--im-vidgrid-min: 11rem">
	{{#each demo.videos}}{{#match @index "<" 5}}{{> "components/video-card" class="im-vidcard-tall" hide_by=true}}{{/match}}{{/each}}
</div>
{{/example}}

<h2>Details that matter</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Autoplay muted, looping, <code>playsinline</code>.</strong> Anything else is blocked, and <code>playsinline</code> is what stops iOS taking the clip full-screen out from under the layout.</li>
	<li><strong>Do not hide the title behind a tap.</strong> It is the only thing telling a viewer what they are looking at.</li>
	<li><strong>One viewport means one viewport.</strong> If the design needs a description, comments and related clips, it is not a short page — it is a <a href="/collections/video/page/">video page</a> with a vertical player.</li>
</ul>
